  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{2}?$",
  "worked_solution": "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(-1,1),(1,1)$ and $(1,-1).$\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,1)}=-{1}^{2}=-1, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,1)}=-{1}^{2}=-1, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,-1)}=-(-1)^{2}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040222/assets/bb078d2e632ef5cc29f0e03903a148c8caf0ded552ab9b2604c5b3ecf586d0c4.png)",
